<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Large scale training implementation details</h2>

<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.00494v1/Methods_new.svg" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/373;" width="476" height="373"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="A1.F5.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Three-pass schedule on a toy sequence.</span>
Example with input “<span id="A1.F5.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abcde</span>” and two augmentation sites at <span id="A1.F5.16" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">b</span> and <span id="A1.F5.17" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">d</span> (<math id="A1.F5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M{=}2</annotation></semantics></math>),
<math id="A1.F5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> latent slots per site (<math id="A1.F5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{b}^{\prime},\texttt{b}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">b</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">b</mtext><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{b}^{\prime},\texttt{b}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.F5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{d}^{\prime},\texttt{d}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">d</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">d</mtext><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{d}^{\prime},\texttt{d}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="A1.F5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{A}{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{A}{=}1</annotation></semantics></math> ahead token per site.
<em id="A1.F5.18" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pass 1</em> (<span id="A1.F5.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Base</span>): run once on the raw sequence to form the cache <math id="A1.F5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="KV_{\theta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KV_{\theta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="A1.F5.20" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pass 2</em> (<span id="A1.F5.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coprocessor</span>): consume <math id="A1.F5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="KV_{\theta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KV_{\theta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> together with the <em id="A1.F5.22" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concatenated</em> <math id="A1.F5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\!\times\!N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\!\times\!N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> latent placeholders <math id="A1.F5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\texttt{b}^{\prime},\,\texttt{b}^{\prime\prime},\,\texttt{d}^{\prime},\,\texttt{d}^{\prime\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">b</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">b</mtext><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">d</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">d</mtext><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\texttt{b}^{\prime},\,\texttt{b}^{\prime\prime},\,\texttt{d}^{\prime},\,\texttt{d}^{\prime\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, producing per-slot latent embeddings <math id="A1.F5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\texttt{b}^{\prime}},z_{\texttt{b}^{\prime\prime}},z_{\texttt{d}^{\prime}},z_{\texttt{d}^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">b</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">b</mtext><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">d</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">d</mtext><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\texttt{b}^{\prime}},z_{\texttt{b}^{\prime\prime}},z_{\texttt{d}^{\prime}},z_{\texttt{d}^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="A1.F5.23" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pass 3</em> (<span id="A1.F5.24" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Base</span> decode): reuse the same <math id="A1.F5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="KV_{\theta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KV_{\theta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and feed the <em id="A1.F5.25" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concatenated</em> outputs plus all <math id="A1.F5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\!\times\!N_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\!\times\!N_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> ahead tokens (one per site in this toy) as input embeddings (e.g., <math id="A1.F5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[z_{\texttt{b}^{\prime}},z_{\texttt{b}^{\prime\prime}},z_{\texttt{d}^{\prime}},z_{\texttt{d}^{\prime\prime}},\hat{\texttt{c}},\hat{\texttt{e}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">b</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">b</mtext><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">d</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">d</mtext><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">c</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">e</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[z_{\texttt{b}^{\prime}},z_{\texttt{b}^{\prime\prime}},z_{\texttt{d}^{\prime}},z_{\texttt{d}^{\prime\prime}},\hat{\texttt{c}},\hat{\texttt{e}}]</annotation></semantics></math>).
Concatenation in Passes 2–3 enables many augmentations per sequence to be trained in parallel while keeping latent computation (Pass 2) separated from next-token prediction (Pass 3).
We are still unsure by the single-pass implementation sketched by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024b)</a></cite> (no public code), but this schedule preserves disentanglement without sacrificing batch parallelism.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.00494v1/Soft_embedding_baseline.svg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/152;" width="238" height="152"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="A1.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Soft-embedding baseline (main control).</span>
A single transformer (no coprocessor) plays both “System-1” and “System-2” roles.
We attach <math id="A1.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> learnable soft tokens (“latent slots”) to the Base and continue pre-training the same Base checkpoint LLM and these tokens on the same dataset and token budget as the dual-model runs.
This matches the dual setup’s latent capacity (<math id="A1.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>) while using half the trainable parameters, directly testing whether a separate coprocessor—and its cache-level latent communication with the Base—provides benefits beyond added compute.
During decoding, the learned soft tokens serve as the latent context via input-embedding injection.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Reasoning implementation details</h2>

<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmarks and curriculum.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al. (2021)</a></cite> and ProsQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al. (2024)</a></cite> we follow the staged curriculum of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
At stage <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the first <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> CoT steps are replaced by <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\times c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\times c</annotation></semantics></math> continuous latents.
Loss is masked on question tokens and on the latent spans; only the remaining language tokens contribute to the objective.
We sweep <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\!\in\!\{1,2,3,4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\!\in\!\{1,2,3,4\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>, so the total latent budget is <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}{=}k\,c\!\in\!\{4,8,12,16\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}{=}k\,c\!\in\!\{4,8,12,16\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
All benchmark fine-tuning runs use a learning rate of <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{\times}10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{\times}10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To make the comparison to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al. (2024)</a></cite> as faithful as possible, we deliberately minimize changes to their curriculum recipe; better schedules may exist for our dual-architecture, but exploring them is out of scope here.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F7.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<p id="A2.F7.fig1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;"><span id="A2.F7.fig1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Notes.<span id="A2.F7.fig1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">
ProsQA is near-saturated for both GPT-2 and Qwen-3, so curves are flat across <math id="A2.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and the soft-embedding baseline tracks Hypothesis 2 closely.
This complements Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Reasoning evaluation: benchmarks and a controlled stress test ‣ 4 Experimental analysis: ‣ Exploring System 1 and 2 communicationfor latent reasoning in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>A (GSM8K), where accuracy likewise fails to grow monotonically with <math id="A2.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Plotting conventions match the main text; dotted lines denote the single-model soft-embedding baseline.</span></span></p><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.00494v1/latent_budget_prosqa.svg" id="A2.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/144;" width="229" height="144"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>ProsQA accuracy vs. total latents <math id="A2.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Countdown setup.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Countdown is trained <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without</em> curriculum.
Operands are sampled uniformly from <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,50]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,50]</annotation></semantics></math> and the target from <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,100]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,100</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,100]</annotation></semantics></math>; expressions use <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\div" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>÷</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\div</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> (as in TinyZero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al. (2025)</a></cite>).
For each <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\text{operands},N_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>operands</mtext><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\text{operands},N_{L})</annotation></semantics></math> setting we train for <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> epochs over <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="262{,}144" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>262,144</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">262{,}144</annotation></semantics></math> generated training samples and evaluate on <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}024</annotation></semantics></math> held-out samples.
We vary the operand count <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\{3,4,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\{3,4,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> and latent budget <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}\!\in\!\{1,2,4,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}\!\in\!\{1,2,4,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
All runs use a learning rate of <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{\times}10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{\times}10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The branching factor grows with operands as <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Catalan}(n{-}1)\,2^{n-1}\,n!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Catalan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Catalan}(n{-}1)\,2^{n-1}\,n!</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the controlled difficulty axis reported in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Reasoning evaluation: benchmarks and a controlled stress test ‣ 4 Experimental analysis: ‣ Exploring System 1 and 2 communicationfor latent reasoning in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>B.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-tuning from large-scale pre-training.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On GSM8K/ProsQA, resuming from large-scale pre-training <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hurts</em> relative to starting from base checkpoints (Tables <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Comparison protocols (all end curriculum at 𝑁_𝐿=16). ‣ Appendix B Reasoning implementation details ‣ Exploring System 1 and 2 communicationfor latent reasoning in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>–<a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Comparison protocols (all end curriculum at 𝑁_𝐿=16). ‣ Appendix B Reasoning implementation details ‣ Exploring System 1 and 2 communicationfor latent reasoning in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), suggesting a mismatch between next-token pre-training geometry and our curriculum-based supervision at this scale.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison protocols (all end curriculum at <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}{=}16</annotation></semantics></math>).</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hyp. 2 (scratch):</span> co-finetune Base and Coprocessor from the standard base checkpoints; staged curriculum with <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> stages and <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> latents per stage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Resume + curriculum:</span> initialize Hyp. 2 from the large-scale training checkpoints (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Large scale data training ‣ 4 Experimental analysis: ‣ Exploring System 1 and 2 communicationfor latent reasoning in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and run the same curriculum (<math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Straight-to-16:</span> initialize from the large-scale checkpoints but skip the curriculum; a single stage with <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c{=}16</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Effect of resuming from large-scale pre-training on <span id="A2.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GSM8K</span>. Accuracy (%); <math id="A2.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the change vs. Hyp. 2 (scratch).</figcaption>
